--TEST--
`Core\Command::help` throws for a name the program declares no `#[Command]` for, and still answers for one it does — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Tools {
    #[Core\Command(name: "build", about: "Compile the project")]
    public static function build(): uint {
        return 0;
    }
}

// § 6 fixes the table while compiling, so a page for a command that does not
// exist is a mistake in the program rather than in its input — a `LogicError`,
// and not an empty page a caller would print to somebody.
try {
    echo "" . Core\Command::help("deploy");
    echo "a page was rendered for a command that does not exist\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal is about the one name: the table still answers for the command
// it does hold, and a command with no argument prints no `arguments:` heading.
echo "" . Core\Command::help("build");
--EXPECT--
`Core\Command::help` was asked for `deploy`, which this program declares no `#[Command]` for
usage: build

Compile the project
